WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:05.670
- Good afternoon, everybody.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.380
Thank you for joining me.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.750
I've just quickly scrolled
through the participants,

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:12.660
and I see some people I
haven't seen in a while

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:14.850
and would love to see again
and some of our colleagues.

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:16.426
So, hello, all of you.

00:00:16.426 --> 00:00:18.810
I wish we were here in person,

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:21.150
but this is our current situation.

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.310
I am going to give a
very short introduction

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.710
to somebody I suspect many of you know,

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:27.330
which is the wonderful Michael Steinlauf,

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:30.120
so as not to take up too much of our time.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.000
So, Michael Steinlauf is
a veteran of many things,

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.890
of Poland in the 1980s,
of Columbia in 1968,

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:41.610
of revolution, of Jewish revival,

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:45.360
and a professor of East
European Jewish History.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.770
We are here today to talk
about a very special book

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:52.320
that has just come out in English

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.380
called, "This Was Not America."

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.450
The book is in a very particular genre,

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.910
which doesn't really exist
on this side of the Atlantic,

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:02.433
but is in fact one of my favorite genres.

00:01:03.439 --> 00:01:06.270
In Polish, you call it a wywiad rzeka,

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:08.100
the kind of river interview

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.610
which is a long dialogue conversation.

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:14.100
In fact, really a kind of
series of conversations

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:18.480
woven together, generally
between two thinkers,

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.100
you know, that turns into a book

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.580
that's not in monological
form, but in dialogical form.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.050
And this book goes through

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.960
a kind of extraordinary autobiography.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:30.840
And at the center of this is a question

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:34.290
about the Holocaust and
Polish-Jewish history.

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:37.320
I will not take up any more time.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:41.250
I will just say that the
book that is beneath the book

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:42.240
we're going to talk about today

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.790
called "Bondage to the
Dead," was one of the books

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:46.470
that had the strongest influences on me

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:48.753
when I was a graduate
student in the 1990s.

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:52.470
I'll tell you how this is going to go.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:55.347
What I'm going to first
ask Professor Steinlauf,

00:01:55.347 --> 00:02:00.060
ask Michael to do, is to
talk a bit about the book,

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:03.690
how it came to be in the
autobiographical narrative,

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:06.540
and then I will prompt
him with some questions.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:08.360
And then I would like to save time

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:12.330
for those of you in the
audience to ask questions.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:16.500
The format for this is
that chat is disabled,

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.910
but the Q&amp;A, Inessa tells me, is enabled,

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:25.050
so if you have questions,
please type them in the Q&amp;A,

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.900
and I will do my best to scan through them

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:31.440
and choose as many as we have time for.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.360
And Michael's a very friendly person.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:37.863
I'm sure he will try his best
to answer everything honestly.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:41.940
Okay, with that, I'm going
to turn that over to Michael

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:45.720
and have him talk to you, tell
you a bit about this book,

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.960
how it came into existence,
and what it's about.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.137
The book is called,
"This Was Not America,"

00:02:52.137 --> 00:02:54.390
and it's a kind of tangled web

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:56.430
of Polish-Jewish American history,

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:01.260
a tangle through Polish-Jewish
American history.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:04.800
- Okay, so I can't talk about this book

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:08.029
because it refers to another book

00:03:08.029 --> 00:03:10.470
that was published 25 years ago,

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:14.727
and that is "Bondage to the Dead,"

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:19.083
Poland and the memory of the Holocaust.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:26.207
I have to refer to it
because this book arose,

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:33.230
the current book arose as a
direct consequence of that book.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:41.343
So, that book was,

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:47.523
well, we have to put
Elżbieta Janicka in here.

00:03:48.377 --> 00:03:51.063
Elżbieta Janicka, my colleague, my friend,

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:57.500
who, at a certain point
some years ago texted me,

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:05.427
saying, "I've read your book seven times."

00:04:08.264 --> 00:04:13.023
And then she says, "It's
unbelievable, I love it.

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:18.980
It's absolutely true. And at
the same time, it's bullshit."

00:04:29.378 --> 00:04:34.378
Now that book, "Bondage to the
Dead," poses the idea that...

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:42.810
See, I was trying to, at
that time 25 years ago,

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:47.340
deal with the fact that how
do you deal with the fact

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:52.340
that years or months
after the end of the war,

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:56.280
people who had hidden Jews
still had to hide them.

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:58.143
What the hell was that? What is that?

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:05.850
I couldn't understand that.

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:10.500
And so I went to the only
place I felt I could go to

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:13.713
is Robert Jay Lifton and
his notion of trauma.

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:16.800
And he was talking about, you know,

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:18.753
things like Hiroshima and so on.

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:24.590
So, this notion of trauma was
then contested by Elżbieta,

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:32.217
who said, "No, no, no,
no. It's not trauma."

00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:38.100
And so we started a conversation.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:41.956
And that conversation
was a kind of, you know,

00:05:41.956 --> 00:05:46.380
as Marcy says, it was a wywiad rzeka.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:47.793
It covered everything.

00:05:48.676 --> 00:05:53.373
And it covered all parts of my life, too.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:56.910
It's everything.

00:05:56.910 --> 00:06:00.003
It's partly biographical,

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:04.200
and it's partly, you know, political.

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:09.200
It's partly me arguing
with her about all of this,

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:13.601
and about the notion of whether trauma,

00:06:13.601 --> 00:06:18.601
first of all, I mean,
mostly, is the proper word,

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:25.160
is the proper concept for
what it is I had encountered.

00:06:27.825 --> 00:06:30.625
And she argued, "No, it's not trauma.

00:06:30.625 --> 00:06:35.625
It's just anti-Semitism,
basically." (chuckling)

00:06:41.698 --> 00:06:42.780
And if people ask me about that,

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:44.962
I can talk about that a little bit.

00:06:44.962 --> 00:06:49.050
But there's many different
directions to go in,

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:51.635
and it was exciting to be able

00:06:51.635 --> 00:06:53.940
to look at all of these things.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:57.180
So, I'm open to, you know, I'll just stop.

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:01.350
And Marci, I mean, do you have something?

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:04.440
I mean, if there's something
that I should have explained

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:05.850
right from the get-go, tell me.

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:06.753
I'll do it.

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:11.853
I'm really open to
whatever, I mean, you know.

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:15.210
- Maybe I'll just throw
a couple things out

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:17.280
for the audience, for the people

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:21.180
who are not already in the
midst of this conversation.

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:24.630
Michael is the child of
survivors of the Holocaust.

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:28.440
Each of his parents had
been married before the war.

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:33.440
You know, each of his parents
lost families during the war,

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:38.220
met and married after the war
as kind of lone survivors.

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:41.850
Michael was born in France after the war.

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:44.040
They soon after moved to Brighton Beach.

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:46.860
He grew up in a Polish-Jewish
community in Brighton Beach,

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:50.425
speaking Polish, hearing
stories about the war.

00:07:50.425 --> 00:07:54.180
He realizes then Brighton
Beach is not actually America,

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:55.200
it's something else.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.503
Everybody speaks Polish and
constantly talks about this war.

00:07:59.981 --> 00:08:02.158
- The little environment

00:08:02.158 --> 00:08:06.420
that I found myself in as a
child was that, but mainly,

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:08.910
and this is not the
Brighton Beach of today.

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:12.510
Because beginning in the '70's,
all these Russians moved in,

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:14.610
and now everything's Russian here.

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:15.903
This is before.

00:08:21.005 --> 00:08:22.650
Yeah, all right, go ahead.

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:25.230
- So, this is childhood in Brighton Beach.

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:27.060
You then grow up,

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:29.660
you have a difficult
relationship with your parents.

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:34.560
You grow up, you go to
Columbia. You're there in 1968,

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:36.480
You're occupying the building.

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:38.880
There's scenes right out
of "Strawberry Statement,"

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:41.130
if anybody remembers that book.

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:43.860
You're a revolutionary,
you move to Seattle,

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:46.110
you try to overthrow
the American government

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:48.090
with a kind of, you know, eclectic mix

00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:52.286
of Marxists and anarchists
and anti-racists and feminists

00:08:52.286 --> 00:08:55.353
and, you know, Black Panthers.

00:08:57.870 --> 00:08:59.100
You go on the run for a while.

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:01.920
So, you have this very,
you're the generation of '68.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:04.239
You have a very 1968 experience.

00:09:04.239 --> 00:09:07.830
You're a revolutionary, you're
a social justice activist.

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:11.220
You go back later, you
know, get a graduate degree

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:13.050
in East European Jewish History.

00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:15.720
Your native language is Polish.

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:19.710
You learn Yiddish, you know,
as a way into this history

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:22.680
that was both yours,
but somehow not quite,

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:24.300
because your parents come from

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:27.540
the assimilated
Polish-speaking intelligentsia.

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:28.710
And then you end up,

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:32.224
after your parents have died,
you know, relatively young,

00:09:32.224 --> 00:09:37.224
in Poland at the end of
martial law around 1983

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:41.940
coming into this scene of
trying to understand the world

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:43.329
that your parents came from,

00:09:43.329 --> 00:09:47.040
you know, in a Warsaw in which
there are not very many Jews.

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:49.037
But there's a handful of them

00:09:49.037 --> 00:09:52.920
who are largely the children
of Polish-Jewish communists

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:55.230
who wanted to have nothing
to do with being Jews,

00:09:55.230 --> 00:09:58.800
who are in the midst of
rediscovering that they are Jews.

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:02.370
And this then becomes your
entry into Poland, you know,

00:10:02.370 --> 00:10:04.291
and your attempt to understand this world

00:10:04.291 --> 00:10:08.940
that disappeared before
you were born, you know,

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:12.090
but that is not gone without a trace

00:10:12.090 --> 00:10:13.645
because through the cracks,

00:10:13.645 --> 00:10:17.040
there are constantly things bubbling up

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:18.813
from this world that was killed.

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:25.516
- Marci, that's wonderful.
Do I need to say more?

00:10:25.516 --> 00:10:27.819
- Thank you. So, this is my
little summary of the book.

00:10:27.819 --> 00:10:30.990
There are a few things in the
book I'd like to talk about.

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:32.297
- Please.

00:10:32.297 --> 00:10:34.500
- But maybe I'll start with the one

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:37.200
that's probably most
important for our audience,

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:40.061
which has to do with this
book, "Bondage to the Dead."

00:10:40.061 --> 00:10:43.053
And that book came out in 1997.

00:10:44.516 --> 00:10:48.180
It came out when I was living in Warsaw,

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:50.520
and I don't remember how
I got a copy in English.

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:52.140
Maybe you brought me a copy in English.

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:54.180
Somehow somebody gave
me a copy of the book

00:10:54.180 --> 00:10:57.095
when I was in Warsaw and
it was not yet easy to get.

00:10:57.095 --> 00:10:58.920
- It may have been me.

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:02.041
- It may have been you,
it might have been Cecile.

00:11:02.041 --> 00:11:03.320
- What year was it?

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:07.590
- It was '97. It was the
year the book came out.

00:11:07.590 --> 00:11:08.940
- Okay. But I wasn't...

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:11.790
I don't think. Oh, it doesn't matter.

00:11:11.790 --> 00:11:13.350
- You were there briefly,

00:11:13.350 --> 00:11:15.780
but various people were
coming back and forth.

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:17.784
In any case, what this book was about,

00:11:17.784 --> 00:11:20.010
you know, was precisely the question

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:21.540
that I had become obsessed with

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:24.450
after two to three months
of living in Warsaw,

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:26.760
which is that, you
know, the Jews are gone,

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:29.040
but there's an obsession with them.

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:30.510
You know, they're gone
and they're not gone.

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:33.090
And there's not only
an obsession with Jews

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:36.630
on behalf of, you know,
a handful of Americans

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:38.880
trying to work through
their identity complexes,

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:41.520
there's also an obsession with the Jews

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:44.100
who are no longer there
on behalf of Poles.

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:45.870
And this absence of the Jews,

00:11:45.870 --> 00:11:47.443
and what has happened to the Jews

00:11:47.443 --> 00:11:51.630
has clearly deeply inflected, you know,

00:11:51.630 --> 00:11:53.460
and weighed upon everything else

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:55.080
that is happening in Poland.

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:57.480
You know, and so I came there in the '90's

00:11:57.480 --> 00:12:00.450
and I'm looking at, there's
all this anti-Semitic graffiti.

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:03.510
There are Stars of David
hanging on gallows.

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:06.420
There are people selling
anti-Semitic books

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:08.610
and "Protocols of the Elders of Zion"

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:11.040
alongside, you know,
books about "Star Wars"

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:14.820
in Polish translation on the street.

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:19.710
- My favorite was a pickup
truck with its back open,

00:12:19.710 --> 00:12:24.710
and on one side was a bunch
of raw meat, you know,

00:12:30.378 --> 00:12:32.700
that they were selling discounted,

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:37.700
and on the other side was
what you said, you know?

00:12:43.710 --> 00:12:47.610
- Yeah, the juxtaposition
was kind of extraordinary,

00:12:47.610 --> 00:12:49.533
because Poland was just opening up.

00:12:50.649 --> 00:12:53.910
- Yeah, on the one hand that
and on the other hand that,

00:12:53.910 --> 00:12:58.623
so, you know, it was a crazy moment.

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:03.787
But I was there, now, are
you talking about '79 now?

00:13:03.787 --> 00:13:05.643
- No, no, I'm talking about '97.

00:13:05.643 --> 00:13:06.476
- '97. Right.

00:13:06.476 --> 00:13:08.714
- I'm talking about '97.
You were there much...

00:13:08.714 --> 00:13:10.020
- When I got there, it wasn't like that.

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:14.157
When I got there, things
were softer and gentler,

00:13:14.157 --> 00:13:16.620
and it wasn't until, you know,

00:13:16.620 --> 00:13:18.903
it was a very different world.

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:25.020
- Yeah, I got there just as
lots of people in the '90's

00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:26.610
were kind of rediscovering

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:28.505
that they might be of Jewish origin,

00:13:28.505 --> 00:13:32.100
that their grandparents were communist,

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:34.920
that perhaps their parents
were Solidarity activists.

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:37.260
Maybe their parents
even had them baptized,

00:13:37.260 --> 00:13:38.403
because at that time,

00:13:39.748 --> 00:13:42.900
to have your child baptized
during the time of Solidarity

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:45.450
when Wojtyła was the patron of solidarity,

00:13:45.450 --> 00:13:47.190
was a sign of freedom.

00:13:47.190 --> 00:13:48.690
And now they were discovering

00:13:48.690 --> 00:13:50.490
that maybe they really had Jewish roots.

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:52.920
It was right when Kostak Gebert,

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:55.170
who was one of the people
who was important for you

00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:56.580
when you came in the '80's,

00:13:56.580 --> 00:14:01.230
had started this new magazine,
Midrasz, you know, in Polish.

00:14:01.230 --> 00:14:05.096
And Kostak was, is one
of the most important

00:14:05.096 --> 00:14:08.582
underground journalism
Solidarity activists,

00:14:08.582 --> 00:14:10.980
who then after, in the '90's,

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:13.913
became Poland's most important
foreign correspondent

00:14:13.913 --> 00:14:18.180
in Bosnia during the War
of Yugoslav Succession.

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:20.670
And also one of the most important actors

00:14:20.670 --> 00:14:21.960
in the Jewish revival,

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:25.110
trying to understand what
it meant to be Jewish.

00:14:25.110 --> 00:14:28.936
And there was this magazine
called Midrasz that still exists

00:14:28.936 --> 00:14:31.710
that he began to edit in 1997.

00:14:31.710 --> 00:14:34.080
- Does it still exist? I heard it closed.

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:37.080
- Oh, it may have closed.
I thought it still existed.

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:39.417
- During the last year, it was gone.

00:14:39.417 --> 00:14:41.190
- Oh, that would be sad.

00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:44.433
In any case, it was basically
aimed at closet Jews.

00:14:45.270 --> 00:14:49.920
And in the second issue, there
was a half-page advertisement

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:52.957
that said, "Do you have Jewish roots?"

00:14:52.957 --> 00:14:55.407
"Czy masz zydowskie
korzenie?" You remember this.

00:14:56.257 --> 00:14:59.640
"Is it a secret?" You
know, "Is it a problem?

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:02.790
Or have you been afraid to
talk to your husband or wife,

00:15:02.790 --> 00:15:04.440
perhaps your children?"

00:15:04.440 --> 00:15:07.890
And it goes on to advertise
a confidential hotline.

00:15:07.890 --> 00:15:10.267
And it says, "Zapewniamy dyskrecję."

00:15:10.267 --> 00:15:11.735
"We guarantee confidentiality."

00:15:11.735 --> 00:15:13.509
- Just like other
confidential (indistinct)

00:15:13.509 --> 00:15:15.850
- Exactly, exactly.

00:15:15.850 --> 00:15:16.683
Just on that model.

00:15:16.683 --> 00:15:19.041
- You know, "Do suspect
you're gay?" I mean...

00:15:19.041 --> 00:15:19.874
- Right.

00:15:19.874 --> 00:15:23.130
- "Do you suspect something
that you can't...?"

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:24.420
And it's an amazing.

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:29.420
We have to understand, you
know, what does it mean,

00:15:30.750 --> 00:15:35.580
you know, that being
Jewish takes that form,

00:15:36.570 --> 00:15:41.570
the revelation that one is of Jewish roots

00:15:43.620 --> 00:15:48.620
has to be approached in
such a crazy, crazy way?

00:15:48.930 --> 00:15:51.480
- It was right after that came out

00:15:51.480 --> 00:15:53.490
when I actually went to see Kostak,

00:15:53.490 --> 00:15:55.440
who I didn't even know at the time,

00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:57.720
and said, "Is this a joke?"

00:15:57.720 --> 00:16:00.210
And he said, "No, it's not a
joke. This is very serious.

00:16:00.210 --> 00:16:02.012
And we get a lot of phone calls."

00:16:02.012 --> 00:16:04.860
And I thought, "What is this about?"

00:16:04.860 --> 00:16:06.420
And that's when your book came out,

00:16:06.420 --> 00:16:07.692
and "Bondage to the Dead" was about,

00:16:07.692 --> 00:16:11.640
and was about what are these complexes

00:16:11.640 --> 00:16:14.760
and layer after layer after layer,

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:17.370
clearly having to do
with a lot of repression,

00:16:17.370 --> 00:16:18.930
you know, a lot of trauma,

00:16:18.930 --> 00:16:21.600
you know, a lot of kind of
hints of Freud here and there.

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:24.874
There were things that you
were trying to figure out.

00:16:24.874 --> 00:16:29.874
Why is Poland so haunted?
Why are Poles so haunted?

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:33.275
You know, why is there this
obsession with the Jews?

00:16:33.275 --> 00:16:35.370
And is it love, and is it hate?

00:16:35.370 --> 00:16:37.590
And is it guilt, and was it, you know,

00:16:37.590 --> 00:16:40.316
participation or was it
passivity, or why is it?

00:16:40.316 --> 00:16:42.360
And these are the
questions that seem to me

00:16:42.360 --> 00:16:45.270
you took on fearlessly in that book

00:16:45.270 --> 00:16:47.640
with an amazing amount of composure.

00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:49.513
You were just trying to figure it out.

00:16:49.513 --> 00:16:50.970
Maybe you could say a little bit

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:53.130
about what you were
thinking as you wrote it.

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:55.323
- As a true American, you see,

00:16:56.190 --> 00:17:01.190
that's the only way I knew
how to approach it, you know?

00:17:01.230 --> 00:17:05.103
Being nice to everybody, making
sure not to insult anybody,

00:17:06.923 --> 00:17:11.400
but trying to figure
this strange thing out.

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:13.470
And then I found out,

00:17:13.470 --> 00:17:17.005
and that's partly where the
title of the book comes from.

00:17:17.005 --> 00:17:18.867
This was not America, you know?

00:17:19.759 --> 00:17:24.759
This was not me, you know,
trying to negotiate something

00:17:26.670 --> 00:17:28.503
that's easily understood,

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:32.580
because it's not something
that's easily understood.

00:17:32.580 --> 00:17:37.020
And why, back then, 25 years ago,

00:17:37.020 --> 00:17:39.270
I was trying to understand why someone

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:44.270
who had hidden Jewish children
still had them after the war,

00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:48.960
still had those children in his garret

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:50.313
and wouldn't let them out.

00:17:51.148 --> 00:17:53.820
What the hell was that?
You know, what is that?

00:17:53.820 --> 00:17:58.350
You know? And that's
insanity in a certain way.

00:17:58.350 --> 00:18:01.350
But if you're Polish, not at all.

00:18:01.350 --> 00:18:04.620
I mean, that's the way a lot of people

00:18:04.620 --> 00:18:06.390
had to deal with this.

00:18:06.390 --> 00:18:11.390
And anyway, so yeah, that's
the inspiration for...

00:18:15.210 --> 00:18:20.210
So my answer back then from
Robert Jay Lifton was trauma.

00:18:21.810 --> 00:18:26.810
They were traumatized by
what they had experienced,

00:18:27.810 --> 00:18:31.683
what they had seen of Jews dying.

00:18:35.070 --> 00:18:36.847
And here comes Elżbieta saying,

00:18:36.847 --> 00:18:40.557
"Nonsense. It's not what it is at all."

00:18:40.557 --> 00:18:42.240
- Can you tell the audience a little bit

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:43.833
about who Elżbieta is?

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:46.380
- You tell.
- (giggles) Okay.

00:18:47.959 --> 00:18:51.420
Because so much of the book
is about that relationship.

00:18:51.420 --> 00:18:54.700
- You think as an academic
and also an artist

00:18:56.250 --> 00:18:59.763
who has written widely about,

00:19:00.780 --> 00:19:05.780
basically focusing most
often on anti-Semitism,

00:19:07.380 --> 00:19:10.537
and the notion that Polish culture,

00:19:20.190 --> 00:19:21.810
I mean, this is the opposite

00:19:21.810 --> 00:19:24.120
of the defensive Polish culture,

00:19:24.120 --> 00:19:29.120
that Polish culture is deeply,
at its roots, anti-Semitic.

00:19:29.910 --> 00:19:32.400
And there's a small number of historians,

00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:34.590
and you know them, of course,

00:19:34.590 --> 00:19:38.490
for whom that is mostly
younger even than Elżbieta,

00:19:38.490 --> 00:19:40.680
who's in her forties or something.

00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:45.680
But, so that was, you know,
that was a bone of contention.

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:54.993
- It's very harsh.
- Huh?

00:19:55.950 --> 00:19:57.297
- She's very harsh in that book.

00:19:57.297 --> 00:20:00.153
- Oh, totally harsh.
Totally harsh, very harsh.

00:20:01.050 --> 00:20:03.116
And I had to cope with that

00:20:03.116 --> 00:20:04.907
(chuckling) as I was writing this book.

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:07.342
And it's all for the good.

00:20:07.342 --> 00:20:09.246
You'll see, I mean, you know,

00:20:09.246 --> 00:20:13.050
those of you who go on from
this and actually read the book,

00:20:13.050 --> 00:20:16.997
you'll see, at least, it's
very interesting places.

00:20:21.900 --> 00:20:26.900
But I'd like to explore,
I mean, this one word,

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:30.507
this word "trauma."

00:20:31.890 --> 00:20:35.318
I know it's very, you told me recently,

00:20:35.318 --> 00:20:38.460
that it's very catchy nowadays,

00:20:38.460 --> 00:20:40.800
and everybody's talking about trauma.

00:20:40.800 --> 00:20:43.290
Well, I mean, I did a lot
of talking about trauma,

00:20:43.290 --> 00:20:45.270
but what is it?

00:20:45.270 --> 00:20:48.524
I mean, I'd love to, you know,

00:20:48.524 --> 00:20:51.437
I mean, does that seem
like a good place to go?

00:20:51.437 --> 00:20:54.240
'Cause I have no idea what trauma is,

00:20:54.240 --> 00:20:56.820
but I use the word all the time.

00:20:56.820 --> 00:20:59.100
- Well, let's disentangle, I would say.

00:20:59.100 --> 00:21:02.880
So you were looking at
collective trauma in this book.

00:21:02.880 --> 00:21:07.880
That the witnessing of people
being murdered in front of you

00:21:08.130 --> 00:21:10.314
was a collective trauma for the Poles.

00:21:10.314 --> 00:21:11.147
- Right. Correct.

00:21:11.147 --> 00:21:13.530
- And today, people might
say secondary trauma,

00:21:13.530 --> 00:21:16.770
when you're watching somebody
else be tortured, you know,

00:21:16.770 --> 00:21:20.280
then you're like vicariously
experiencing something.

00:21:20.280 --> 00:21:22.380
And the other thing that is very kind of,

00:21:22.380 --> 00:21:24.930
that is very, "popular"
isn't the right word,

00:21:24.930 --> 00:21:27.030
but that we keep returning to a lot now,

00:21:27.030 --> 00:21:30.219
is intergenerational
trauma, which in some sense,

00:21:30.219 --> 00:21:32.700
your book is about all
three of these things.

00:21:32.700 --> 00:21:35.010
Collective trauma,
(chuckles) secondary trauma,

00:21:35.010 --> 00:21:37.264
and intergenerational trauma.

00:21:37.264 --> 00:21:38.664
- Who knew? Who knew, right?

00:21:40.872 --> 00:21:44.340
You know, (chuckles) once again,

00:21:44.340 --> 00:21:47.820
I found myself by accident
getting into the most,

00:21:47.820 --> 00:21:50.460
you know, kind of just
completely by accident,

00:21:50.460 --> 00:21:55.460
getting into the trendiest
kind of moments, you know?

00:21:57.890 --> 00:22:00.499
- So you're very fashionable.

00:22:00.499 --> 00:22:01.800
- I'm very fashionable.

00:22:01.800 --> 00:22:02.760
That's nice to know.

00:22:02.760 --> 00:22:06.180
I'm pretty old, so it's nice
to be fashionable still.

00:22:06.180 --> 00:22:07.620
- I mean, you kept trying to,

00:22:07.620 --> 00:22:11.269
for me, your book was
about trying to understand.

00:22:11.269 --> 00:22:12.200
- Yes.

00:22:12.200 --> 00:22:15.540
- And for me, it was a
model of what historians do

00:22:15.540 --> 00:22:17.310
is we try to understand.

00:22:17.310 --> 00:22:19.544
You know, before we condemn or judge,

00:22:19.544 --> 00:22:22.200
we try to make that imaginative leap

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:25.050
and understand what was this phenomenon,

00:22:25.050 --> 00:22:26.817
what was happening, you know?

00:22:26.817 --> 00:22:29.280
And you come to this idea of trauma,

00:22:29.280 --> 00:22:31.410
and Elżbieta's like, "Trauma, shmauma.

00:22:31.410 --> 00:22:33.398
They're all anti-Semites."

00:22:33.398 --> 00:22:35.798
- (chuckles) That's
precisely correct, you know?

00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:37.973
It isn't, yeah.

00:22:39.752 --> 00:22:44.752
The root of everything is,
and one exchange that we had,

00:22:46.950 --> 00:22:48.600
and with, I forget who it was.

00:22:48.600 --> 00:22:50.220
It wasn't directly with her,

00:22:50.220 --> 00:22:54.727
but how could people have
done this to people, right,

00:23:00.823 --> 00:23:02.402
was like a question.

00:23:02.402 --> 00:23:05.910
And she said, "No,
that's not the question."

00:23:05.910 --> 00:23:10.910
It's like, "People did
this to Jews, not people."

00:23:12.750 --> 00:23:15.960
And so that for me said a lot.

00:23:15.960 --> 00:23:18.300
That, for me, helped me to understand.

00:23:18.300 --> 00:23:20.670
And is that too elliptical?

00:23:20.670 --> 00:23:21.877
But you know what I...

00:23:21.877 --> 00:23:23.520
I think people can understand.

00:23:23.520 --> 00:23:24.614
- Well, let me now,

00:23:24.614 --> 00:23:26.730
I mean, one of the
questions your book raises,

00:23:26.730 --> 00:23:29.670
and I, as an American,
you know, and as a Jew,

00:23:29.670 --> 00:23:32.309
and someone who's spent
a lot of time in Poland,

00:23:32.309 --> 00:23:35.520
you spent a lot of time, you know,

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:39.720
with your cohort of 1968 in America,

00:23:39.720 --> 00:23:43.050
with activists for gay rights,
with feminist activists,

00:23:43.050 --> 00:23:44.433
with Black activists.

00:23:46.012 --> 00:23:49.638
You know, you're coming from a
certain kind of cosmopolitan,

00:23:49.638 --> 00:23:53.580
internationalist sensitivity,

00:23:53.580 --> 00:23:56.730
in which suffering,
victimization, scapegoating,

00:23:56.730 --> 00:24:00.120
is not something that
happens only to Jews.

00:24:00.120 --> 00:24:00.953
- Right.

00:24:00.953 --> 00:24:04.020
- That must have inflected
how you related to Poland

00:24:04.020 --> 00:24:05.040
when you got there,

00:24:05.040 --> 00:24:06.607
because you're coming from an America

00:24:06.607 --> 00:24:09.330
in which racism, first and foremost,

00:24:09.330 --> 00:24:12.404
is directed not against
Jews, but against Blacks.

00:24:12.404 --> 00:24:13.458
- Right. Right.

00:24:13.458 --> 00:24:18.458
And here it's all Jews.
So, you know, I don't know.

00:24:19.230 --> 00:24:22.423
Is that...? (chuckles)

00:24:23.280 --> 00:24:26.040
- How did your experiences
in America, then,

00:24:26.040 --> 00:24:29.610
working with American racism
and trying to understand it

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:32.673
inflect how you understood
Polish anti-Semitism?

00:24:33.540 --> 00:24:34.503
- How did it?

00:24:35.640 --> 00:24:39.750
Well, I thought, you
know, I came believing

00:24:39.750 --> 00:24:42.873
that I could, you know,
transpose these experiences,

00:24:45.009 --> 00:24:48.483
you know, directly into
the Polish situation.

00:24:54.516 --> 00:24:58.599
But then I was told, "No,
no, no. This is not so.

00:25:02.100 --> 00:25:06.240
This is a very different situation,

00:25:06.240 --> 00:25:09.597
a very different history, a
very different everything."

00:25:10.440 --> 00:25:13.080
And I came, you know, kind of naively

00:25:13.080 --> 00:25:15.350
with this kind of naive American...

00:25:16.560 --> 00:25:21.183
What's that book by Henry
James, where she comes to...

00:25:21.183 --> 00:25:22.433
- Oh, right, right. Yeah.

00:25:23.347 --> 00:25:24.510
- "Golden Bowl." It's "The
Golden Bowl," I think.

00:25:24.510 --> 00:25:25.343
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:25:25.343 --> 00:25:27.240
You know, where she just...

00:25:27.240 --> 00:25:29.407
You know, I came thinking,

00:25:29.407 --> 00:25:33.067
"Oh, you know, here's
another freedom struggle."

00:25:33.067 --> 00:25:36.210
Right? Another freedom struggle.

00:25:36.210 --> 00:25:39.537
And that's the way I have to approach it,

00:25:39.537 --> 00:25:42.093
you know, in a very personal way,

00:25:43.350 --> 00:25:47.910
because, you know, and then it turned out,

00:25:47.910 --> 00:25:50.550
you know, as everything in
Europe, (chuckles) it turns out,

00:25:50.550 --> 00:25:53.283
certainly in Poland, above all in Poland,

00:25:54.300 --> 00:25:59.300
no, as they say, (speaking Polish) right?

00:26:04.096 --> 00:26:05.611
(Marci laughing)

00:26:05.611 --> 00:26:08.400
It's layered. It's layered.

00:26:08.400 --> 00:26:12.180
There's so many things that you don't get.

00:26:12.180 --> 00:26:14.940
And it took me a while to figure that out.

00:26:14.940 --> 00:26:19.940
But, you know, is that
reasonable, to say that?

00:26:20.790 --> 00:26:21.800
I mean...

00:26:23.340 --> 00:26:25.830
- That it's a very complicated question?

00:26:25.830 --> 00:26:28.920
No, I found it an enormously
complicated question.

00:26:28.920 --> 00:26:31.170
I mean, when I read your book,

00:26:31.170 --> 00:26:34.050
I felt like you ask
all the right questions

00:26:34.050 --> 00:26:35.970
and you articulated them so well,

00:26:35.970 --> 00:26:37.518
and you gave the right examples,

00:26:37.518 --> 00:26:39.240
but in the end, you didn't...

00:26:39.240 --> 00:26:40.950
Right. There was no ultimate answer.

00:26:40.950 --> 00:26:44.130
But I empathized with like,

00:26:44.130 --> 00:26:45.900
yes, that was the right question.

00:26:45.900 --> 00:26:50.900
And there's something there
we haven't quite gotten to.

00:26:51.150 --> 00:26:53.130
- Exactly. Exactly.

00:26:53.130 --> 00:26:56.563
And I still haven't, you know?

00:26:56.563 --> 00:27:00.751
It's not, you know, I'm
an old man now, (chuckles)

00:27:00.751 --> 00:27:02.843
but has wisdom accompanied that?

00:27:02.843 --> 00:27:05.520
- I mean, the paradox in the book is that,

00:27:05.520 --> 00:27:07.440
you know, you're there
as the American Jew,

00:27:07.440 --> 00:27:10.140
and Elżbieta is a very self-critical Pole,

00:27:10.140 --> 00:27:12.616
and she's gaslighting
you, (chuckles) you know,

00:27:12.616 --> 00:27:14.010
through the whole second half of the book.

00:27:14.010 --> 00:27:16.567
Everything you say, you know, she's like,

00:27:16.567 --> 00:27:18.090
"But you can't really think that,"

00:27:18.090 --> 00:27:19.290
or, "You're repressing that,'

00:27:19.290 --> 00:27:20.610
or "You're trying not to,"

00:27:20.610 --> 00:27:23.000
or "You're doing something circumlocutious

00:27:23.000 --> 00:27:25.410
so as to excuse people
who shouldn't be excused."

00:27:25.410 --> 00:27:26.460
And...

00:27:26.460 --> 00:27:28.633
- I'm still trying to
understand, by the way.

00:27:28.633 --> 00:27:33.633
There's words popping up that
I'm still learning how to use.

00:27:34.357 --> 00:27:36.960
"Gaslighting," is one of them.

00:27:36.960 --> 00:27:39.770
You know, it's not clear
to me what all that...

00:27:39.770 --> 00:27:44.770
You know, I finally come
closer to the notion of trauma.

00:27:44.940 --> 00:27:48.210
But gaslighting is still
pretty far from my...

00:27:48.210 --> 00:27:50.907
- Gaslighting is new. I've
only recently used it.

00:27:50.907 --> 00:27:52.110
But I'm using it now

00:27:52.110 --> 00:27:57.110
in the sense of she's trying
to cause you to doubt yourself.

00:27:57.994 --> 00:27:58.827
- Right. Right.

00:27:58.827 --> 00:28:00.176
- Constantly critiquing
you in a way that you start

00:28:00.176 --> 00:28:01.343
to doubt your own...
- Well, as an American Jew,

00:28:01.343 --> 00:28:03.213
- I mean, you know, what else?

00:28:04.290 --> 00:28:08.370
You know, any American Jewish comedians,

00:28:08.370 --> 00:28:10.710
you know, will agree.

00:28:10.710 --> 00:28:13.413
Yeah, what is this? I don't know.

00:28:14.605 --> 00:28:16.863
And she knows, you know?

00:28:19.770 --> 00:28:24.770
I have an Israeli friend who
was in the States for a while,

00:28:26.160 --> 00:28:29.200
and maybe you know, him, Yoav Peled

00:28:33.496 --> 00:28:36.420
And I went up to, he
was staying in a loft,

00:28:36.420 --> 00:28:39.510
way up high in Chinatown.

00:28:39.510 --> 00:28:42.510
And I went up to see him,
and it was a really hot day.

00:28:42.510 --> 00:28:44.882
And, and I said, "Yoav."

00:28:44.882 --> 00:28:47.730
This tells you something about Israelis.

00:28:47.730 --> 00:28:51.780
I said, "Yoav, I just don't
get it. I don't understand."

00:28:51.780 --> 00:28:54.780
And to which he responded,
"You don't understand?

00:28:54.780 --> 00:28:58.447
I will tell you."

00:28:58.447 --> 00:29:00.753
You know, there's only one answer.

00:29:01.680 --> 00:29:03.787
There's only one answer, at least, so.

00:29:07.100 --> 00:29:09.420
- So the other thing
interesting in that story

00:29:09.420 --> 00:29:11.550
is that, you know, part of this story

00:29:11.550 --> 00:29:15.210
is part of an international
generation of 1968.

00:29:15.210 --> 00:29:17.700
You know, on both sides
of the Iron Curtain

00:29:17.700 --> 00:29:19.525
and on both sides of the Atlantic,

00:29:19.525 --> 00:29:21.990
there was an Oedipal rebellion.

00:29:21.990 --> 00:29:23.820
There was a revolt against the parents,

00:29:23.820 --> 00:29:27.447
against the silences and
complicities of the parents.

00:29:27.447 --> 00:29:29.315
And one of those things

00:29:29.315 --> 00:29:32.807
that, you know, in your case
is you have these parents

00:29:33.912 --> 00:29:36.270
who are coming from the
Polish-Jewish intelligentsia,

00:29:36.270 --> 00:29:38.790
who are coming from middle class families.

00:29:38.790 --> 00:29:42.540
They speak perfect literary
Polish, and they hate the Poles.

00:29:42.540 --> 00:29:44.640
They have nothing good
to say about the Poles.

00:29:44.640 --> 00:29:46.987
You spent your childhood
listening to this word,

00:29:46.987 --> 00:29:50.010
"Wojna, wojna, wojna," you
know, which is interesting,

00:29:50.010 --> 00:29:52.200
'cause it's precisely the
word in Russian, "war,"

00:29:52.200 --> 00:29:54.313
which is banned now in Russia,

00:29:54.313 --> 00:29:57.150
in which everything was subsumed under.

00:29:57.150 --> 00:29:58.980
They have nothing good
to say about the Poles.

00:29:58.980 --> 00:30:00.977
They don't wanna, like,
the Poles all hated them.

00:30:00.977 --> 00:30:03.540
Sometime you tell this story in the book

00:30:03.540 --> 00:30:06.000
where you're out with your
parents on the street,

00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:08.767
somebody hears them speaking
Polish, and they're like,

00:30:08.767 --> 00:30:11.427
"Ah, rodacy!" Like,
"Compatriots!" you know?

00:30:11.427 --> 00:30:14.220
And your father's like, "Ah,
rodacy. To hell with you."

00:30:14.220 --> 00:30:15.300
Like, we're not the...

00:30:15.300 --> 00:30:17.760
- Runs away. And runs away.

00:30:17.760 --> 00:30:22.760
Takes his family, you know,
like protecting us, you know?

00:30:23.492 --> 00:30:25.797
Like, "Let's get away from this person."

00:30:26.996 --> 00:30:30.772
"Rodak." Countryman.

00:30:30.772 --> 00:30:33.479
And he just said, "Ja mu dam rodak."

00:30:33.479 --> 00:30:35.119
"I'll give him rodak."

00:30:35.119 --> 00:30:37.619
And it felt like he was
gonna punch him, you know?

00:30:38.517 --> 00:30:39.900
- And you go to Poland.

00:30:39.900 --> 00:30:43.740
And you go to Poland to
commune with a Jewish path,

00:30:43.740 --> 00:30:46.500
but you also go to Poland
with an openness to Poles.

00:30:46.500 --> 00:30:49.470
You go to Poland with a
willingness to speak Polish.

00:30:49.470 --> 00:30:51.690
You know, you go to
Poland with a willingness

00:30:51.690 --> 00:30:52.726
to try to understand.

00:30:52.726 --> 00:30:55.357
- They gave me this
language, so I better use it.

00:30:55.357 --> 00:30:58.195
I mean, I was very lucky, you know?

00:30:58.195 --> 00:30:59.910
They gave me, you know, and they lied.

00:30:59.910 --> 00:31:03.900
They said so I could communicate.

00:31:03.900 --> 00:31:08.400
See, I was the only kid in
Brooklyn, in that situation,

00:31:08.400 --> 00:31:13.400
who had an active knowledge of Polish.

00:31:16.454 --> 00:31:18.660
You know, all the kids like
me, they could understand it,

00:31:18.660 --> 00:31:20.430
but they couldn't speak it, so.

00:31:20.430 --> 00:31:22.440
- That's amazing. Why was that?

00:31:23.509 --> 00:31:25.710
- (sighs) You know what they told me?

00:31:25.710 --> 00:31:27.840
They told me so that I could speak

00:31:27.840 --> 00:31:30.033
with my aunt and uncle in Paris.

00:31:31.140 --> 00:31:35.313
My uncle was with my mother's
older brother. They survived.

00:31:36.330 --> 00:31:38.970
And that's what they said,
so that you could speak to...

00:31:38.970 --> 00:31:43.020
And not because, you know, it was worthy,

00:31:43.020 --> 00:31:45.243
even that it was a
worthy language. Nothing.

00:31:46.230 --> 00:31:49.533
That's what I grew up being told.

00:31:51.376 --> 00:31:54.510
- And you, I mean, when
I see you in Poland,

00:31:54.510 --> 00:31:56.229
you know, I feel like one
of the things we share

00:31:56.229 --> 00:31:58.560
is we both love Poland.

00:31:58.560 --> 00:32:02.520
Not because we don't see
what's horrible about Poland,

00:32:02.520 --> 00:32:04.530
but there's a deep love there.

00:32:04.530 --> 00:32:06.423
- There is.
- And a deep attachment.

00:32:07.260 --> 00:32:08.103
- There is.

00:32:09.570 --> 00:32:11.010
It's a beautiful place

00:32:11.010 --> 00:32:14.790
where people keep asking
fascinating questions,

00:32:14.790 --> 00:32:18.933
and, you know, I mean,
(chuckles) they're a minority,

00:32:20.582 --> 00:32:24.376
but some of them, (chuckles) you know?

00:32:24.376 --> 00:32:27.570
And nowadays they're there.
Of course they're there.

00:32:27.570 --> 00:32:29.130
Who knows? There's gonna be elections.

00:32:29.130 --> 00:32:30.870
Maybe, maybe. Who knows?

00:32:30.870 --> 00:32:32.223
Keep your fingers crossed.

00:32:33.330 --> 00:32:35.543
Maybe the good guys will win, huh?

00:32:35.543 --> 00:32:38.340
- And how much of that
reaching out to the Poles

00:32:38.340 --> 00:32:40.510
was a rebellion against your parents?

00:32:43.383 --> 00:32:47.818
- I'm not sure. I'm not sure.

00:32:47.818 --> 00:32:51.117
Yeah, I mean, I rebelled against
my parents in lots of ways.

00:32:51.117 --> 00:32:53.284
I'm not sure that was one.

00:32:56.091 --> 00:32:59.231
'Cause my father was long
gone by then, you know?

00:32:59.231 --> 00:33:01.051
He died 10 years before.

00:33:01.051 --> 00:33:03.634
And I'm not sure, I'm not sure.

00:33:08.010 --> 00:33:13.010
It was an intense desire
to see what the world...

00:33:14.220 --> 00:33:19.220
See, it turned out wrong
for me, personally.

00:33:19.568 --> 00:33:22.110
I didn't want to talk about
the Holocaust, you know?

00:33:22.110 --> 00:33:25.115
I wanted to talk about the
world before the Holocaust.

00:33:25.115 --> 00:33:26.760
You know, I wanted to talk about life.

00:33:26.760 --> 00:33:29.257
I mean, this is the
way I talked back then.

00:33:29.257 --> 00:33:31.980
"I wanna talk about life,
not death," you know?

00:33:31.980 --> 00:33:36.980
I wanna, you know, find out
what this world was about

00:33:37.200 --> 00:33:40.160
that my parents, and it seemed
like a fascinating world.

00:33:40.160 --> 00:33:45.160
And so I became enamored of who?

00:33:47.250 --> 00:33:50.730
Of dead Jews, you see.

00:33:50.730 --> 00:33:53.808
And people ask me, "Well,
you know, what is the...?"

00:33:53.808 --> 00:33:58.808
I mean, you know,
everyone has like a group.

00:33:59.400 --> 00:34:04.400
Everyone has someone who they
define themselves against.

00:34:09.540 --> 00:34:13.203
And, you know, I have this,

00:34:14.460 --> 00:34:17.699
I think I tell the story in the book,

00:34:17.699 --> 00:34:22.699
about people, about these Chicanos.

00:34:24.180 --> 00:34:25.950
You don't use the word Chicanos anymore,

00:34:25.950 --> 00:34:28.000
but back then, we used the word Chicanos,

00:34:29.430 --> 00:34:34.297
Mexican Americans, who talked
about how they had this land

00:34:45.900 --> 00:34:50.900
that's today northern New
Mexico, rather northern Mexico

00:34:56.921 --> 00:34:59.340
and southern New Mexico,

00:34:59.340 --> 00:35:01.937
that the mythical name for it is Aztlán.

00:35:03.570 --> 00:35:04.620
Aztlán, A-Z-T-L-A-N.

00:35:04.620 --> 00:35:07.590
It's a beautiful concept.
It's a beautiful thing.

00:35:07.590 --> 00:35:09.960
And I got really into
that back in Seattle,

00:35:09.960 --> 00:35:13.380
because a lot of these
people talked about Aztlán.

00:35:13.380 --> 00:35:16.590
And then I suddenly realized,
(chuckles) you know,

00:35:16.590 --> 00:35:21.590
that Aztlán is like the world

00:35:21.720 --> 00:35:22.703
that (speaking Yiddish) comes from,

00:35:24.734 --> 00:35:26.670
you know, because it's at the same time,

00:35:26.670 --> 00:35:29.590
it's beautiful, you know?

00:35:29.590 --> 00:35:34.590
And at the same time, it's both
beautiful and impoverished,

00:35:34.590 --> 00:35:36.720
totally impoverished, you know?

00:35:36.720 --> 00:35:37.980
And who are these Jews?

00:35:37.980 --> 00:35:39.660
You know, where are these Jews from?

00:35:39.660 --> 00:35:41.100
You know, and it's funny,

00:35:41.100 --> 00:35:44.190
'cause that's not who my parents
were, of course. (chuckles)

00:35:44.190 --> 00:35:49.190
But this notion of, "Yeah, we are Jews,

00:35:54.630 --> 00:35:58.290
and this is our homeland, okay?"

00:35:58.290 --> 00:36:00.723
And we all have homelands, of course.

00:36:02.340 --> 00:36:04.754
But my homeland, when I went back there,

00:36:04.754 --> 00:36:07.323
was dead Jews, it turned out, so.

00:36:09.150 --> 00:36:12.868
- But you also met some live Jews.

00:36:12.868 --> 00:36:17.868
and Stajek and Krejewski
and Kostak Gebert and Anika.

00:36:18.471 --> 00:36:22.230
- Yeah, and Paweł Śpiewak,
who just passed on.

00:36:22.230 --> 00:36:26.343
A wonderful guy, a wonderful man.

00:36:27.210 --> 00:36:29.190
You know, he was head

00:36:29.190 --> 00:36:33.360
of the Jewish Historical
Institute for some years.

00:36:33.360 --> 00:36:36.480
Anyway. Yeah, yeah.

00:36:36.480 --> 00:36:38.649
And some of these people were,

00:36:38.649 --> 00:36:43.649
you know, I think they
saw me as totally naive.

00:36:43.650 --> 00:36:48.270
I remember Paweł saying to
me at one point, you know,

00:36:48.270 --> 00:36:52.320
because I was into running
back then, you know,

00:36:52.320 --> 00:36:57.320
and no one, you know, people
would sic their dogs on me.

00:36:57.496 --> 00:36:59.431
(Marci laughing)

00:36:59.431 --> 00:37:04.431
You know, I was especially, I
liked in the, what was that?

00:37:04.628 --> 00:37:07.461
In the Royal Gardens. What's that?

00:37:08.608 --> 00:37:09.633
- Łazienki.

00:37:09.633 --> 00:37:12.128
- Łazienki. I used to love
to run through Łazienki.

00:37:12.128 --> 00:37:13.954
- I love running in Łazienki.

00:37:13.954 --> 00:37:17.817
- Well, you were, you know,
I was out there, you know,

00:37:17.817 --> 00:37:22.817
and people would sic their dogs
on me, you know, and laugh.

00:37:23.610 --> 00:37:24.690
And they even laughed.

00:37:24.690 --> 00:37:26.760
They would say, "Well, what
the hell is this guy doing?

00:37:26.760 --> 00:37:28.647
You know, running?"

00:37:29.550 --> 00:37:31.653
So, yeah.

00:37:36.210 --> 00:37:38.910
What was that an answer to? I've lost it.

00:37:38.910 --> 00:37:41.160
- Oh, the live Jews who you met,

00:37:41.160 --> 00:37:43.830
who were working through
what it meant to be Jewish.

00:37:43.830 --> 00:37:44.961
- Yeah, yeah.

00:37:44.961 --> 00:37:47.160
So Paweł said, so when he heard it,

00:37:47.160 --> 00:37:49.477
when I told him about that, he said,

00:37:49.477 --> 00:37:53.450
"Oh, the American wants to
live forever," you know?

00:37:55.657 --> 00:37:57.750
"The American... Yeah, I get it.

00:37:57.750 --> 00:38:01.080
So the American... Yeah."

00:38:01.080 --> 00:38:03.660
- And what did you learn from them?

00:38:03.660 --> 00:38:05.340
How did that group of people

00:38:05.340 --> 00:38:08.010
who were connected to Solidarity,

00:38:08.010 --> 00:38:11.460
who were carrying out a Jewish revival,

00:38:11.460 --> 00:38:14.560
who did not grow up in that Jewish,

00:38:14.560 --> 00:38:17.280
specifically, traditionally Jewish milieu,

00:38:17.280 --> 00:38:19.440
and were trying to discover it as adults,

00:38:19.440 --> 00:38:21.570
what did you learn from them?

00:38:21.570 --> 00:38:22.830
- Well, learn from them.

00:38:22.830 --> 00:38:24.721
I mean, they were just like me, you see,

00:38:24.721 --> 00:38:26.250
because I didn't, you know,

00:38:26.250 --> 00:38:30.289
I didn't grow up speaking
Yiddish, you know?

00:38:30.289 --> 00:38:33.633
I wanted to have, I wished I had.

00:38:34.590 --> 00:38:39.590
So in a certain way, that
brought me very close to them.

00:38:42.480 --> 00:38:45.420
And on the other hand, you
know, they were digging.

00:38:45.420 --> 00:38:50.010
They were digging, and they
were creating something new.

00:38:50.010 --> 00:38:54.807
And that was really
extraordinary, you know?

00:39:00.750 --> 00:39:05.750
I mean, what did I learn from them?

00:39:07.050 --> 00:39:12.050
You know, it's hard to
answer that question,

00:39:12.180 --> 00:39:13.350
you know, what did I learn?

00:39:13.350 --> 00:39:16.860
- 'Cause they were a certain
kind of mediating bridge.

00:39:16.860 --> 00:39:19.162
There were the dead
Jews who you never met.

00:39:19.162 --> 00:39:22.860
There were the Jews in Brighton
Beach who you grew up with.

00:39:22.860 --> 00:39:27.300
And then there were these
Jews who were your generation,

00:39:27.300 --> 00:39:31.140
who you were encountering
in this place where,

00:39:31.140 --> 00:39:33.660
had things been different,
you might have been them.

00:39:33.660 --> 00:39:35.490
You might have grown up there.

00:39:35.490 --> 00:39:37.830
- Oh, easily, easily. That's right.

00:39:37.830 --> 00:39:40.353
That's right. So, I don't know.

00:39:42.491 --> 00:39:44.640
I mean, I've often thought
I could have been them,

00:39:44.640 --> 00:39:48.095
but I also could have grown
up in Israel, you know?

00:39:48.095 --> 00:39:52.292
My father had tickets to go there.

00:39:52.292 --> 00:39:54.630
I mean, he was given, this
guy gave him, you know,

00:39:54.630 --> 00:39:57.090
and he, my father thought,
"Hmm, should we go?

00:39:57.090 --> 00:39:59.040
Nah, we won't go."

00:39:59.040 --> 00:40:00.810
I mean, you know, it was that...

00:40:00.810 --> 00:40:03.060
I sometimes consider my life, you know,

00:40:03.060 --> 00:40:08.060
just being, if only, if only, you know?

00:40:09.840 --> 00:40:14.103
There's, I mean, not only
did I not have a homeland,

00:40:15.780 --> 00:40:20.780
but who were these people, you know?

00:40:22.860 --> 00:40:24.180
They were Polish Jews,

00:40:24.180 --> 00:40:25.860
but they also could have been Israelis,

00:40:25.860 --> 00:40:28.413
I mean, in that way, you know?

00:40:29.430 --> 00:40:30.930
Anyway, I think I'm...

00:40:30.930 --> 00:40:32.760
Yeah, does that make sense?

00:40:32.760 --> 00:40:33.900
- It does.

00:40:33.900 --> 00:40:37.013
And you've spent a lot
of your life, you know,

00:40:37.013 --> 00:40:40.440
working through a relationship

00:40:40.440 --> 00:40:45.261
with a brother who died
before you were born.

00:40:45.261 --> 00:40:46.094
- Yes.

00:40:48.000 --> 00:40:50.973
- Why don't you, I'll let you
tell the audience this story.

00:40:52.080 --> 00:40:55.023
- The story is a story.

00:40:56.730 --> 00:41:01.713
My father (chuckles) had the
extraordinary good fortune,

00:41:02.876 --> 00:41:05.283
you know, Jews love irony,

00:41:06.141 --> 00:41:09.850
the extraordinary good
fortune to have gotten married

00:41:10.995 --> 00:41:15.483
at the end of 1939 in Warsaw.

00:41:19.756 --> 00:41:22.680
And shortly thereafter, you know,

00:41:22.680 --> 00:41:27.680
they immediately made a
child, he and this woman.

00:41:27.780 --> 00:41:30.063
Not my mother, I mean, he and this woman.

00:41:34.890 --> 00:41:39.890
And as far as I can tell, at
the age of this boy, Michał,

00:41:45.330 --> 00:41:46.380
they called him Michał,

00:41:46.380 --> 00:41:49.050
but there's all these different words,

00:41:49.050 --> 00:41:52.323
because he was named after
my grandfather, Moisha,

00:41:53.250 --> 00:41:54.393
as was I.

00:41:56.370 --> 00:41:58.893
Or was I named after him?
That was never clear.

00:42:00.510 --> 00:42:05.510
But at the age of roughly
two-and-a-half, he was killed.

00:42:05.940 --> 00:42:07.560
He died.

00:42:07.560 --> 00:42:12.560
Now, it was Elżbieta who
helped me really understand

00:42:12.870 --> 00:42:15.033
the most probable way he died.

00:42:16.200 --> 00:42:20.700
And the most probable way he
died was they were hiding.

00:42:20.700 --> 00:42:24.240
My father was gone. My father
was not in their hiding place.

00:42:24.240 --> 00:42:29.240
And we're talking 1942, right, after July,

00:42:30.330 --> 00:42:32.133
after the Aktion and all that.

00:42:35.156 --> 00:42:40.156
And so how did this happen?

00:42:44.490 --> 00:42:47.343
Well, most probably, of course, who knows?

00:42:48.210 --> 00:42:53.010
You know, they were in a
hiding place, and the child,

00:42:53.010 --> 00:42:57.090
who was about two-and-a-half,
made some sounds, you know?

00:42:57.090 --> 00:43:02.090
And his mom was holding
him, and his mom killed him.

00:43:02.130 --> 00:43:03.423
His mom smothered him.

00:43:04.290 --> 00:43:09.230
That was a documented, you know...

00:43:10.404 --> 00:43:14.290
There's enough documentation
of stuff like this

00:43:15.750 --> 00:43:18.450
to make that the most probable.

00:43:18.450 --> 00:43:21.480
Although not, you know, some
Nazi could have grabbed him

00:43:21.480 --> 00:43:23.340
and smashed his head against the wall.

00:43:23.340 --> 00:43:26.760
That's possible too. I've
thought of that sometimes.

00:43:26.760 --> 00:43:29.343
I mean, you know, not nice.

00:43:31.621 --> 00:43:35.190
And at a certain point, (chuckles)

00:43:35.190 --> 00:43:37.260
yeah, you told me, you
warned me against dybbuk,

00:43:37.260 --> 00:43:42.260
but I'll say at a certain point
late, you know, in my life,

00:43:44.760 --> 00:43:46.599
I mean, relatively late.

00:43:46.599 --> 00:43:51.599
I was living, my friends were printers

00:43:51.930 --> 00:43:54.510
who printed for the "revolution,"

00:43:54.510 --> 00:43:59.510
and I was staying at their
place on the Lower East Side.

00:44:00.750 --> 00:44:05.750
And it felt that inside
me was something strange,

00:44:11.010 --> 00:44:12.900
was something that's...

00:44:12.900 --> 00:44:15.279
Now, you have to understand,
you know, dybbuks,

00:44:15.279 --> 00:44:18.540
you know, there are dybbuks and dybbuks.

00:44:18.540 --> 00:44:21.033
There's the nice dybbuk of the play,

00:44:25.920 --> 00:44:28.620
but then there's bad dybbuks,

00:44:28.620 --> 00:44:30.720
which is what they usually are.

00:44:30.720 --> 00:44:33.727
Like, I read about one
dybbuk, someone asked him,

00:44:33.727 --> 00:44:35.647
"Why'd you come back?"

00:44:35.647 --> 00:44:39.796
"Well, I came back, you
know, I had to come back

00:44:39.796 --> 00:44:42.813
because I slept with boys," you know?

00:44:43.650 --> 00:44:46.560
And, you know, so there's
usually something,

00:44:46.560 --> 00:44:50.919
except in the play, there's
usually something bad associated

00:44:50.919 --> 00:44:53.460
that they have to redress.

00:44:53.460 --> 00:44:58.460
And how do you redress a
two-and-a-half-year-old child?

00:45:00.150 --> 00:45:05.150
And that's what I experienced.
I experienced rage.

00:45:05.700 --> 00:45:10.700
Rage, because, you know,
"Why did you do this to me?

00:45:10.830 --> 00:45:11.970
How did I die?"

00:45:11.970 --> 00:45:16.970
You know? "What was this? What was this?"

00:45:19.620 --> 00:45:22.921
And so, you know, and it was in my belly,

00:45:22.921 --> 00:45:26.643
this kind of turning.

00:45:31.350 --> 00:45:34.263
Yeah, turning is really
the best word for it.

00:45:35.740 --> 00:45:38.667
You told me not to, but
I'm talking about it.

00:45:38.667 --> 00:45:40.050
But I'm done, I think.

00:45:40.050 --> 00:45:43.590
I mean, it's very, very...

00:45:43.590 --> 00:45:48.590
And it pursued me. I mean he pursued me.

00:45:49.140 --> 00:45:50.613
I mean, for a while.

00:45:53.640 --> 00:45:56.487
I guess it was a sense of, you know,

00:45:58.699 --> 00:46:03.699
"Why did you," meaning plural,
"why did you do this to me?"

00:46:06.000 --> 00:46:07.741
How did this happen?" you know?

00:46:07.741 --> 00:46:11.007
And it's like this rage,
this kind of, you know,

00:46:15.799 --> 00:46:20.799
two-year-old-rage, you know,
what that might be like.

00:46:21.766 --> 00:46:22.599
I mean, you have kids.
(Marci laughing)

00:46:22.599 --> 00:46:27.350
I had two-year-old rage,
"Because you killed me."

00:46:31.410 --> 00:46:34.047
Anyway, so there you go.

00:46:34.047 --> 00:46:39.047
And that came out of, and
that's in this book too.

00:46:39.420 --> 00:46:40.980
- Oh, there's a lot
about him in this book.

00:46:40.980 --> 00:46:43.953
And I was at the
performance in Krasnogruda,

00:46:45.793 --> 00:46:48.280
that maybe, which was extraordinary.

00:46:48.280 --> 00:46:50.040
Maybe you could tell the audience

00:46:50.040 --> 00:46:54.870
about this kind of musical
artistic resolution.

00:46:54.870 --> 00:46:59.820
- Yeah, that was what sent
him back to wherever he...

00:46:59.820 --> 00:47:02.030
That performance, I mean, was...

00:47:04.200 --> 00:47:06.900
We created this performance
with the, what's his name,

00:47:07.961 --> 00:47:09.270
with the musician?

00:47:09.270 --> 00:47:10.103
What's his name?

00:47:11.959 --> 00:47:13.740
- Was it Michał?

00:47:13.740 --> 00:47:15.663
- No, it wasn't Michał.

00:47:15.663 --> 00:47:17.810
No, it was someone... (sighs)

00:47:18.930 --> 00:47:20.010
Oh, he's wonderful.

00:47:20.010 --> 00:47:21.322
He's a wonderful...

00:47:21.322 --> 00:47:23.400
- I know you know who
you're talking about.

00:47:23.400 --> 00:47:26.361
I'll look it up. You keep talking.

00:47:26.361 --> 00:47:27.194
- Yeah. All right.

00:47:27.194 --> 00:47:29.970
But anyway, and the of two of us,

00:47:29.970 --> 00:47:34.353
he did the music, and I did
various texts that I dug up

00:47:38.880 --> 00:47:43.880
about trying to deal
with this dybbuk memory.

00:47:50.138 --> 00:47:55.138
And it was like a performance
we did at Krasnogruda.

00:47:57.517 --> 00:48:01.620
And, oh, what's his name?

00:48:01.620 --> 00:48:04.503
He's still performing.
He's a wonderful musician.

00:48:05.670 --> 00:48:09.453
He's a solo guitar player,
among other things.

00:48:12.690 --> 00:48:17.690
And so, anyway, this
performance at Krasnogruda,

00:48:18.420 --> 00:48:22.920
Krosnogruda is just is a
wonderful, is in a magical place

00:48:22.920 --> 00:48:27.920
in the far northeast of Poland
where magical things happen,

00:48:33.072 --> 00:48:33.905
(Marci giggles)

00:48:33.905 --> 00:48:36.420
and one of them was this.

00:48:36.420 --> 00:48:39.179
And they have, you know,
they bring people together

00:48:39.179 --> 00:48:42.540
to talk about the stuff
we're talking about, partly,

00:48:42.540 --> 00:48:46.206
but years and years later than, you know,

00:48:46.206 --> 00:48:48.423
what we've been talking about here.

00:48:49.650 --> 00:48:51.450
But anyway, that years and years later

00:48:51.450 --> 00:48:55.140
is what allowed me to let go of,

00:48:55.140 --> 00:49:00.089
or maybe he (chuckles)
allowed himself to let go.

00:49:00.089 --> 00:49:02.639
Who knows? Maybe, you know,
but something happened.

00:49:03.660 --> 00:49:06.630
I'm no longer possessed, thank goodness.

00:49:06.630 --> 00:49:10.650
- But you were very
empathetic to that possession

00:49:10.650 --> 00:49:12.300
or that intimacy, I mean...

00:49:12.300 --> 00:49:14.288
- How can you not be empathetic?

00:49:14.288 --> 00:49:16.173
A two-and-a-half-year-old. Come on.

00:49:17.231 --> 00:49:20.190
I mean, what am I supposed
to, you know, curse him?

00:49:20.190 --> 00:49:22.190
It's enough that he cursed me, you know?

00:49:24.660 --> 00:49:29.073
- No, Elżbieta's, you
know, probable scenario.

00:49:35.070 --> 00:49:36.690
- Hey, where are you?

00:49:36.690 --> 00:49:41.600
Hey, hey, hey, hey. Hey, hey, hey.

00:49:42.930 --> 00:49:43.763
Hey.

00:49:46.440 --> 00:49:47.610
Hey.

00:49:47.610 --> 00:49:49.353
Technical, technical.

00:49:52.140 --> 00:49:52.973
Are you back?

00:49:52.973 --> 00:49:54.570
- Yeah, I'm here. (giggles)

00:49:54.570 --> 00:49:56.700
- Hey. Yay.
- Hello.

00:49:56.700 --> 00:49:58.380
- Okay, what was I gonna say?

00:49:58.380 --> 00:50:01.209
- Okay, so, no, the scene of the death,

00:50:01.209 --> 00:50:03.450
'cause Michał Głowiński, who is another,

00:50:03.450 --> 00:50:06.660
who was a child also in the Warsaw ghetto

00:50:06.660 --> 00:50:09.347
and wrote a book around the
same time in the nineties,

00:50:09.347 --> 00:50:12.450
"Czarne Sezony" which I then
translated into English.

00:50:12.450 --> 00:50:15.270
And there's a scene he
describes, you know,

00:50:15.270 --> 00:50:20.270
of a baby in a cellar
where everybody is hiding.

00:50:20.280 --> 00:50:22.016
They don't wanna be taken
for the deportations.

00:50:22.016 --> 00:50:24.780
The baby keeps making noise.

00:50:24.780 --> 00:50:26.970
People are saying, "You
have to smother the baby,

00:50:26.970 --> 00:50:29.010
otherwise we're all gonna be caught."

00:50:29.010 --> 00:50:31.860
I use that scene in my
undergraduate seminars,

00:50:31.860 --> 00:50:35.130
and I say, "You know, what are
the ethics of this situation?

00:50:35.130 --> 00:50:37.020
Do you smother the baby?"

00:50:37.020 --> 00:50:40.920
You know, there are no innocent
choices in this scenario.

00:50:40.920 --> 00:50:42.813
- No, of course not. And you do.

00:50:42.813 --> 00:50:46.875
But, I have to say, my
situation was even tougher,

00:50:46.875 --> 00:50:49.484
because this was not a baby.
- Right.

00:50:49.484 --> 00:50:52.260
- This was, you know, a kid

00:50:52.260 --> 00:50:56.403
who already knew some language, you know?

00:50:57.690 --> 00:51:01.148
So that's, I would just add that.

00:51:01.148 --> 00:51:01.981
And yeah.

00:51:06.270 --> 00:51:09.600
- And you say in the book that
when your first son was born,

00:51:09.600 --> 00:51:13.007
you policed his birth. (chuckles)

00:51:13.007 --> 00:51:13.840
(Michael chuckling)

00:51:13.840 --> 00:51:16.440
So that there would be no further dybbuks.

00:51:16.440 --> 00:51:20.703
- I was with my wife who was,
you know, we were together.

00:51:23.400 --> 00:51:24.510
I mean, we were together.

00:51:24.510 --> 00:51:27.870
She was giving birth and I
was watching her over her,

00:51:27.870 --> 00:51:30.807
and she was out of it, you know?

00:51:34.957 --> 00:51:36.872
You know, she's out of it.

00:51:36.872 --> 00:51:41.370
This child is coming out,
this baby's coming out.

00:51:41.370 --> 00:51:45.120
And I did something, you know?

00:51:45.120 --> 00:51:47.740
I just wanted to make sure

00:51:48.780 --> 00:51:52.186
that there wasn't anything around

00:51:52.186 --> 00:51:55.260
that was gonna grab into him, you know,

00:51:55.260 --> 00:51:57.573
because that would be a logical thing.

00:51:58.680 --> 00:52:03.680
Dybbuk, you know, moves outta
me and goes into my son.

00:52:05.982 --> 00:52:09.390
(chuckles) I did it this
little ritual or something.

00:52:09.390 --> 00:52:11.130
I don't even remember really what,

00:52:11.130 --> 00:52:13.740
but that was a concern of mine.

00:52:13.740 --> 00:52:15.600
I said, "You're not getting this one.

00:52:15.600 --> 00:52:18.720
You're not getting this one. Uh huh."

00:52:18.720 --> 00:52:22.621
So this is, I talk about
generational, you know?

00:52:22.621 --> 00:52:23.454
- Yeah.

00:52:23.454 --> 00:52:25.330
- And this is generational.
- Yeah.

00:52:25.330 --> 00:52:28.950
- No, a lot of, I mean, a
lot of our questions now

00:52:28.950 --> 00:52:33.330
about what led to this
anthropological catastrophe in Russia

00:52:33.330 --> 00:52:36.330
have kind of gone back to
intergenerational trauma.

00:52:36.330 --> 00:52:39.000
The kind of, the idea of
things that were not processed,

00:52:39.000 --> 00:52:40.683
that were not talked about,

00:52:41.599 --> 00:52:42.450
- In Russia. Yeah.

00:52:42.450 --> 00:52:46.140
- Right, that keep like
experiences under Stalinism

00:52:46.140 --> 00:52:47.790
that were silent,

00:52:47.790 --> 00:52:51.380
that one generation passed
on to the next generation.

00:52:51.380 --> 00:52:55.500
- There's nothing, I mean,
that's going on in Russia today

00:52:55.500 --> 00:52:58.909
that can compare to what
happened under Stalinism.

00:52:58.909 --> 00:53:03.909
You know, it's not
proportional. It's not right.

00:53:05.106 --> 00:53:07.530
I mean, it's 3 million, what is it?

00:53:07.530 --> 00:53:12.530
3 million Ukrainians starved
to death, purposely? (chuckles)

00:53:13.442 --> 00:53:15.843
You know, I think that,
you know, but anyway.

00:53:16.897 --> 00:53:20.460
- The idea is that what is
not somehow worked through

00:53:20.460 --> 00:53:23.790
or spoken about, or that there's some kind

00:53:23.790 --> 00:53:26.213
of intergenerational
transmission of trauma, you know?

00:53:28.810 --> 00:53:29.643
- Okay.

00:53:29.643 --> 00:53:33.300
- That's been, I think, where
a lot of the discussion is.

00:53:33.300 --> 00:53:34.380
We're running out of time,

00:53:34.380 --> 00:53:36.723
but I wanna get to some of the questions.

00:53:37.830 --> 00:53:39.300
You know, one of them,

00:53:39.300 --> 00:53:42.297
the first one from Alexandra Kolojeska,

00:53:43.290 --> 00:53:46.627
I think I know the answer to,
but I will let you answer it.

00:53:46.627 --> 00:53:50.370
"Was it possible to detach the personal

00:53:50.370 --> 00:53:53.851
and generational trauma
from the academic aspect

00:53:53.851 --> 00:53:57.600
while you are writing
and researching books?

00:53:57.600 --> 00:54:00.584
Did you feel like you could
somehow compartmentalize

00:54:00.584 --> 00:54:04.830
your own biography and
your family relations

00:54:04.830 --> 00:54:07.287
as you were being a scholar in Poland?"

00:54:10.680 --> 00:54:14.673
- (sighs) Short answer, no.

00:54:17.250 --> 00:54:19.530
There's, you know, I could discuss stuff.

00:54:19.530 --> 00:54:23.413
I mean, there's more to
it, obviously, but no.

00:54:29.310 --> 00:54:34.310
I mean, my being in Poland
was all about, you know,

00:54:35.670 --> 00:54:37.893
going where my parents had been,

00:54:39.150 --> 00:54:42.150
I mean, and feeling the feelings.

00:54:42.150 --> 00:54:46.087
And, you know, for example, Nalewki.

00:54:51.420 --> 00:54:56.420
Nalewki, that's where, I
mean, before the war, Nalewki,

00:54:57.300 --> 00:55:01.114
not today's Nalewki, but
before the war Nalewki,

00:55:01.114 --> 00:55:06.114
was where my mother lived and
where they had their business.

00:55:10.020 --> 00:55:14.760
And I would go, you know, the
addresses were all screwed up.

00:55:14.760 --> 00:55:18.693
So, but I wasn't, yeah.

00:55:21.630 --> 00:55:24.060
You see, because
originally I went to Poland

00:55:24.060 --> 00:55:26.403
to work on Yitskhok Peretz,

00:55:27.654 --> 00:55:32.654
you know, the great Yiddish
writer, the great Yiddish sage.

00:55:36.750 --> 00:55:38.160
And I dropped that,

00:55:38.160 --> 00:55:41.163
because I thought I needed
to write about Poland.

00:55:42.060 --> 00:55:46.083
So, anyway.

00:55:47.100 --> 00:55:52.100
- Oh, somebody here saying
it's Raphael Rogiński

00:55:52.920 --> 00:55:55.413
- Oh, okay. Hi.

00:55:56.820 --> 00:55:58.500
- No, who was the musician.

00:55:58.500 --> 00:56:00.540
- The person talking to you like this,

00:56:00.540 --> 00:56:04.350
this is hearsay from somebody I...

00:56:04.350 --> 00:56:07.830
- No, no, that's who I was
thinking of too, was Raphael.

00:56:07.830 --> 00:56:11.130
I think it was Raphael
Rogiński at that performance

00:56:11.130 --> 00:56:12.600
in Krasnogruda.

00:56:12.600 --> 00:56:17.600
- Ah, oh, is that who's talking now?

00:56:17.610 --> 00:56:21.360
- No, somebody else wrote in questions

00:56:21.360 --> 00:56:23.910
that that was the name of musician.

00:56:23.910 --> 00:56:26.880
No, that's the person I
have that I'm thinking of.

00:56:26.880 --> 00:56:29.460
- And I sure wish he was out
there, but I don't think he is.

00:56:29.460 --> 00:56:31.710
- Yeah. No, I don't think
he's on Zoom right now.

00:56:31.710 --> 00:56:35.100
But no, this is the name I
also forgot, but I know like...

00:56:35.100 --> 00:56:36.390
- Wonderful. I can't praise him.

00:56:36.390 --> 00:56:38.043
I mean, he is a wonderful guy.

00:56:39.990 --> 00:56:41.280
- No, it was extraordinary.

00:56:41.280 --> 00:56:44.015
Okay, let me give you one more question.

00:56:44.015 --> 00:56:47.147
This is a question about,

00:56:47.147 --> 00:56:51.810
there are other children of
survivors who grew up hearing,

00:56:51.810 --> 00:56:55.350
you know, "Never again," and,
"The Poles are anti-Semites,"

00:56:55.350 --> 00:56:58.047
you know, and, "That's a graveyard."

00:56:59.190 --> 00:57:03.990
How would you speak to other children

00:57:03.990 --> 00:57:07.105
whose parents were survivors,
or grandchildren of survivors?

00:57:07.105 --> 00:57:09.420
Should they go back to Poland?

00:57:09.420 --> 00:57:11.006
How would you encourage them?
How would you explain it?

00:57:11.006 --> 00:57:12.480
- Absolutely I would encourage them.

00:57:12.480 --> 00:57:17.400
Let go. Let go of all your
bullshit, of all your hatreds.

00:57:17.400 --> 00:57:18.233
You know, really.

00:57:18.233 --> 00:57:20.580
I mean, you know, 'cause people like me

00:57:20.580 --> 00:57:24.960
were instructed in hatred of Poles.

00:57:24.960 --> 00:57:29.853
I mean, and no.

00:57:32.503 --> 00:57:34.830
Look, back when I was in Poland,

00:57:34.830 --> 00:57:37.690
it was a place that was
a little easier to like

00:57:38.849 --> 00:57:41.130
than the Poland of today.

00:57:41.130 --> 00:57:46.130
So, you know, with its right-wing
nationalist everything,

00:57:49.650 --> 00:57:53.190
but yeah.

00:57:53.190 --> 00:57:55.740
But you will find, go to Poland.

00:57:55.740 --> 00:57:58.230
You'll find people you
can talk to about it,

00:57:58.230 --> 00:58:00.303
and people that are fascinating.

00:58:01.590 --> 00:58:02.970
Yeah.

00:58:02.970 --> 00:58:05.340
- I mean, now there's a
right-wing government,

00:58:05.340 --> 00:58:07.590
but it's still a divided society.

00:58:07.590 --> 00:58:11.670
There's still that, like, there's
still that half of Poland.

00:58:11.670 --> 00:58:13.736
- Half of, well, it's not...

00:58:13.736 --> 00:58:18.060
See, what I explain to people
is that it's just like us.

00:58:18.060 --> 00:58:19.920
- Right. Exactly.

00:58:19.920 --> 00:58:22.080
- But there's more of the bad guys

00:58:22.080 --> 00:58:23.793
and here there's more of the good guys.

00:58:23.793 --> 00:58:28.290
- (laughing) Yeah, the
percentage kind of like...

00:58:28.290 --> 00:58:31.740
Yeah, the percentage is
a topic for discussion,

00:58:31.740 --> 00:58:34.200
But we could also like ask,

00:58:34.200 --> 00:58:37.410
look at how Poland has
taken in Ukrainian refugees.

00:58:37.410 --> 00:58:38.460
It's a moment.

00:58:38.460 --> 00:58:41.760
- Yes, yes. Absolutely, absolutely.

00:58:41.760 --> 00:58:46.020
I mean, God, it is beautiful.

00:58:46.020 --> 00:58:49.050
It's beautiful, and it shows, you know...

00:58:49.050 --> 00:58:51.090
And then, you know, every
now and then, you know,

00:58:51.090 --> 00:58:54.240
because I have a Black son,

00:58:54.240 --> 00:58:56.377
every now and then, you know, I think,

00:58:56.377 --> 00:59:00.450
"What if they were African?" (chuckles)

00:59:00.450 --> 00:59:01.283
How quickly...?

00:59:01.283 --> 00:59:04.170
But that's neither here
or there. But, you know.

00:59:04.170 --> 00:59:06.210
- And you brought him to Poland.

00:59:06.210 --> 00:59:07.043
- What?

00:59:07.043 --> 00:59:08.220
- You brought your son to Poland.

00:59:09.404 --> 00:59:14.404
- Yeah, but it's not
exactly a place for him.

00:59:14.735 --> 00:59:17.628
- No.
- Especially not now.

00:59:17.628 --> 00:59:20.494
- No.

00:59:20.494 --> 00:59:21.327
- No.

00:59:26.490 --> 00:59:28.110
There's so many pieces of my life

00:59:28.110 --> 00:59:32.093
that are unintegrated still, that, yeah.

00:59:35.760 --> 00:59:37.290
- We're just about out of time,

00:59:37.290 --> 00:59:39.360
but I wanna give you a chance,

00:59:39.360 --> 00:59:42.570
like having gone through
this, it's a remarkable book.

00:59:42.570 --> 00:59:45.330
I wanna recommend. It's very easy to read.

00:59:45.330 --> 00:59:47.190
It's very conversational.

00:59:47.190 --> 00:59:50.700
You know, it's not, there
are scholarly references,

00:59:50.700 --> 00:59:53.040
but it's not stilted in academic,

00:59:53.040 --> 00:59:56.233
you know, it's very kind of contestatory,

00:59:56.233 --> 00:59:57.840
you know, and passionate in places.

00:59:57.840 --> 01:00:02.100
But it's also about searching
for a way forward, you know?

01:00:02.100 --> 01:00:07.100
And it's about trying from
all of this trauma, you know,

01:00:07.440 --> 01:00:09.870
to see, is there a light?

01:00:09.870 --> 01:00:13.020
Is there a way forward? Is
there a way we can understand?

01:00:13.020 --> 01:00:18.020
- Oh that's where my origins
in the sixties comes in,

01:00:18.956 --> 01:00:22.470
you know, because nowadays,
everything's darker, you know?

01:00:22.470 --> 01:00:24.750
And for the young people, too.

01:00:24.750 --> 01:00:29.750
And that's so sad, because
back then it had to get better.

01:00:30.000 --> 01:00:31.680
And what's more, you were there

01:00:31.680 --> 01:00:33.870
to make sure it's gonna get better.

01:00:33.870 --> 01:00:37.398
That's what the sixties were, you know?

01:00:37.398 --> 01:00:42.398
And today, it's problematic,
that kind of attitudes.

01:00:46.320 --> 01:00:49.250
Yeah, but I was totally...

01:00:50.910 --> 01:00:55.283
I believed that despite everything,
we, whatever that meant,

01:00:58.269 --> 01:00:59.937
we would prevail, you know?

01:01:05.400 --> 01:01:10.400
So it would be nice if it
was the opposite, you know?

01:01:10.920 --> 01:01:14.310
If I had started in a very negative place,

01:01:14.310 --> 01:01:19.310
and then in my age, now,
you know what I'm saying,

01:01:20.640 --> 01:01:23.523
if I was vindicated in some way.

01:01:24.540 --> 01:01:29.356
But unfortunately things are
just getting darker. (chuckles)

01:01:29.356 --> 01:01:32.853
- Oh. How do you feel now
about our potential to prevail?

01:01:34.050 --> 01:01:36.090
- How do I feel now about what?

01:01:36.090 --> 01:01:38.370
- Our potential to prevail.

01:01:38.370 --> 01:01:39.203
- Potential?

01:01:39.203 --> 01:01:43.013
Well, you know, maybe you
can beat the Russians.

01:01:45.840 --> 01:01:48.060
I mean, you know, maybe the Ukrainians,

01:01:48.060 --> 01:01:49.710
maybe they can do it.

01:01:49.710 --> 01:01:51.660
I mean, that's not out of the question.

01:01:52.560 --> 01:01:55.950
God knows. But that would be incredible.

01:01:55.950 --> 01:01:58.440
And that would be kind of the thing

01:01:58.440 --> 01:02:00.750
we were dreaming of back then.

01:02:00.750 --> 01:02:04.866
I mean, you know, the
forces of evil beaten down.

01:02:04.866 --> 01:02:06.333
I don't know.

